{"text":"𝐅𝐚𝐜𝐮𝐥𝐭𝐲 𝐫𝐞𝐯𝐢𝐞𝐰𝐬: 𝐌𝐦𝐁, 𝐒𝐒𝐀𝟐, 𝐌𝐤𝐮𝐧, 𝐌𝐓𝐒𝐀\nMmB (MIS107): As you all know, this course can feel quite boring, but MmB sir does a great job of making it interesting and easy to understand. He explains every topic very clearly and uploads all his lecture slides on his own website right at the beginning of the semester which is extremely helpful for preparation. He takes 3 quizzes and 2 midterms, but the quizzes are conducted together with the midterm and final exams on the same syllabus. In the midterm, the first 15 minutes are allocated for 20 MCQs(quiz) followed by the written exam. The written section includes 2 short questions and 1 broad case-based question. The final exam follows the similar pattern, with 4 short questions and 1 broad case-based question. One very helpful aspect is that he gives clear exam suggestions beforehand, mentioning which slides are important for quizzes, short questions, and case-based questions. The only difficulty I faced was time management during exams. He usually gives around 1 hour and 10 minutes to complete both the MCQs and the written portion, which can feel quite tight—especially in the final exam, it felt extremely challenging to manage time properly and write 5 questions with enough detail. Apart from this, he genuinely tries his best to make the course as easy as possible. He takes two presentations. One is individually marked, where you only need to prepare presentation slides (no report). The other is group-based, which requires both slides and report. All slides and reports must be submitted on Canvas and the group report also needs to be submitted as a hard copy. Additionally, he conducts 4 lab classes and 1 lab exam, which is very easy as everything comes directly from what he teaches during the lab sessions. His marking is quite lenient, and his grading is fair. From my experience, he is a very friendly and easygoing faculty who gives generous marks. If you take proper notes and go through all the slides thoroughly, you can easily secure an A/A- in this course.\nOverall rating: 9.5/10\nSSA2 (ENG105): He is one of the sweetest faculties you’ll ever meet. Sir is extremely helpful, explains every topic very clearly, provides proper handouts and makes students practise a lot during class. He genuinely wants everyone to do well in the course. The only ‘Pera’ he gives is during research topic selection—apart from that, he is a very easygoing faculty. He takes 4 quizzes, all of them are super easy. The midterm exam is centrally conducted, and the questions are also easy and come directly from what he teaches during lectures. He takes an easy presentation on your research project. Regarding the research paper, he is very strict about AI usage (as most ENG105 faculties are). If you blindly use AI and copy-paste from it, you will get zero. However, he encourages students to take help from AI to generate ideas and learn, just not to copy blindly. All papers are submitted through Turnitin, so AI use is easily detected. From my experience, his grading is fair, he marks leniently and he may curve slightly I guess. If you follow his instructions properly, you can definitely secure an A/A– in this course. This guy also makes the class quite interesting—you’ll never feel bored even if your class is at 8:00 am (we had an 8 am class).\nOverall rating: 9.5/10\nMkun (CHE203): Sir is a very kind faculty who explains all topics clearly. He writes out the structures of amino acids, carbohydrates, lipids, nucleic acids during lectures which makes memorisation much easier. The only issue is that his handwriting is not always easy to understand. He takes 4 quizzes and counts the best 3. The quizzes include MCQs and structure-based questions and he also gives assignments focused only on structures. He takes one midterm, which includes MCQs, short “explain why” questions and structures. The final exam follows the same pattern. He curves a lot, his marking is lenient, and he gives partial marks. If you do proper self-study, go through his slides thoroughly and take notes on whatever he emphasises during lectures (such as which structures or mechanisms are important), it becomes much easier to secure an A/A– in this course. And please don’t be late to class—he will literally scream at you if you arrive even 1 minute late.\nOverall rating: 9/10\nMTSA (BBT312L & BBT314L): Each of these lab courses contains 4 experiments. Sir teaches well but he expects your basics to be clear, as most of the core concepts are already covered in the theory courses—He said. During lab sessions, he mainly gives a brief overview of the topics rather than teaching everything from scratch. All lab reports have to be submitted via Canvas. He takes a viva where he tests your basic concepts, how well you can connect ideas and how each experiment is related to the others. In the final exam, nothing comes directly from the slides—the slides are only for guidance. Every question is conceptual. He often mentions some of the questions during lab sessions, so if you take proper notes, it becomes much easier to answer them. The exam pattern includes 5 MCQs, 5 T/F questions, followed by short conceptual questions and finally a linking question where you have to explain how these 3/4 experiments are connected to each other. He uploads lab report marks on Canvas and he usually gives almost full marks, unless you make major mistakes in the report or submit it late. Overall, if your concepts are clear and you stay attentive during lab sessions, you can easily secure an A/A– in these labs under him. \nOverall rating: 8.5/10 See less","image_alt":[]}